  "query_preview": "..An upper-level low continues to progress eastward\nacross Arizona this evening, eventually phasing with Hurricane\nPolo overnight into Wednesday morning. As these system phase\ntogether, a deformation axis develops across the Four Corners\nregion, combining with wrap-around moisture to result in an \ninvigoration in rain showers across this area. High resolution \nguidance indicates most of the activity will be focused along and \nsoutheast of Glen Canyon. A general lack of instability will\nlikely keep rainfall in this area more stratiform in nature,\nthough isolated embedded heavier cores are possible especially\nin areas near the Arizona border early Wednesday morning. \n\nNorthwesterly flow developing on the heels of the exiting system\nushers in a much drier airmass across Utah and southwest Wyoming\non Wednesday. A high pressure ridge is expected to strengthen\nacross the western US through the remainder of the week, resulting\nin limited precipitation chances and a gradual warming trend\nthrough the weekend and into early next week. Highs are expected\nto peak on Monday around 10 degrees above seasonal averages.\n\nA bit more uncertainty in the forecast exists early next week,\nwith around 20% of ensemble members suggesting the potential for a\nshortwave digging into the region to bring increased moisture\nacross southern Utah that could bring more active weather.\nThe Eastern Pacific remains very active with several tropical \ndisturbances and systems already in place; the eventual\nprogression of these systems will heavily impact how our forecast\nevolves over the next week.",
